author rju -- Thu, 28 Aug 2014 10:31:05 +0200
<p>True. These checks cannot be generated automatically from the "model". We have to specify that separately either in a separate model or in Java code. For some tests this has already been done. A possible concept would be a Kieker record file with each record present in the way it is now in the specs. We should use the text format for records to be able to read it. </p>

<p>On that basis we can perform at least syntactic checks on all record types. Semantic checks cannot be done. For example if we have a record</p>
<div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="preformattedContent panelContent">
<pre>entity A {
   string left
   string right
   int count
}
</pre>
</div></div>
<p>and that gets changed to</p>
<div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="preformattedContent panelContent">
<pre>entity A {
   string right
   string left
   int count
}
</pre>
</div></div>
<p>then a test checking that there are two strings will not find the ordering problem. That is also the problem with this present bug. This can only be checked semantically or by directly checking the name of the property. So my proposed solution would not work for the particular problem noted here first.</p>

<p>Therefore, we need a separate specification which includes the names and types of all properties in a specified order.</p>

<p>For example we could use code like the following:</p>
<div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="preformattedContent panelContent">
<pre>public void testRecord() {
   Record record = new Record (P_BLUE,P_GREEN,P_RED);

   Assert.assertTrue ("'blue' value assertion failed.", record.getBlue(), P_BLUE);
   Assert.assertTrue ("'green' value assertion failed.", record.getGreen(), P_GREEN);
   Assert.assertTrue ("'red' value assertion failed.", record.getRed(), P_RED);

   ByteBuffer buffer = createByteBuffer(P_BLUE,P_GREEN,P_RED);
   Record record = new Record (buffer, strings);

   Assert.assertTrue ("'blue' value assertion failed.", record.getBlue(), P_BLUE);
   Assert.assertTrue ("'green' value assertion failed.", record.getGreen(), P_GREEN);
   Assert.assertTrue ("'red' value assertion failed.", record.getRed(), P_RED);
}
</pre>
</div></div>

<p>This must be done by hand. </p>

