author rju -- Tue, 10 Mar 2020 15:29:25 +0100
<p>The key issue was that the log4j configuration file was not included and not referenced with each tool. I have fixed that by adding three new parts to application <tt>build.gradle</tt> files.</p>

<p>Additional JVM arguments:</p>
<div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="codeContent panelContent">
<pre class="code-groovy">
applicationDefaultJvmArgs = [<span class="code-quote">"-Dlog4j.configuration=file:<span class="code-comment">///#APP_HOME/log4j.cfg"</span>]</span>
</pre>
</div></div>

<p>Rewriting the start scripts of the application:</p>
<div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="codeContent panelContent">
<pre class="code-groovy">
startScripts {
    doLast {
        unixScript.text = unixScript.text.replace(<span class="code-quote">'#APP_HOME'</span>, <span class="code-quote">'$APP_HOME'</span>) 
        windowsScript.text = windowsScript.text.replace(<span class="code-quote">'#APP_HOME'</span>, <span class="code-quote">'%PORT%'</span>) <span class="code-comment">//untested
</span>    }
}
</pre>
</div></div>
<p>This is a hack, but it was recommended by the developer of the application gradle plugin.</p>

<p>Adding the log4j.cfg file to the application directory.</p>
<div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"><div class="codeContent panelContent">
<pre class="code-groovy">
